<h1>Partner with AI to Write Quality Code</h1>

<p class="byline">David Hyland-Wood</p>

<p class="standfirst">How I build software with Claude: a pipeline of narrow agents, a pass that deletes what should never have existed, and a ranked report that helps humans clean up AI slop.</p>

<p>A large language model can write two thousand plausible lines of code per minute. That is simultaneously a grand technological achievement and a way to automate yourself into a huge mess.</p>

<p>Nobody reviews two thousand lines carefully. Anyone who says they do is either skimming or lying. Now try it with 100,000 lines. A colleague recently dropped 160,000 lines on me. I&rsquo;m just not going to review that.</p>

<p>We know development can be done quickly, even amazingly quickly. That&rsquo;s the realm of AI. What you want and how you want it to look when it is done are still the domain of people. AI influencers will tell you all about those things, <a href="https://claude.com/blog/the-ai-native-sdlc-playbook">as has Anthropic</a>.</p>

<p>There is another, and arguably more important, place for people in our use of strong AI to create software: It is up to us to tell the technology how to create good code.</p>

<p>What do I mean by good code? I mean that it complies with a specification, that it has low cyclometric complexity, that I can read it if I need to, that it reuses functions instead of reimplementing near copies, that it is, in short, maintainable by whatever we will use in the future.</p>

<hr>

<h2>The workflow</h2>

