<hr>

<p>I build in three stages:</p>

<h2>Stage 01 &mdash; Build with gates</h2>

<p>My <a href="https://github.com/prototypo/claude-dev-pipeline">Claude development pipeline</a> runs six agents with one job each. A project manager, developer, code reviewer, security reviewer, tester, and documenter. The constraints matter more than the roles.</p>

<ul>
  <li>The project manager orchestrates and <strong>never writes code</strong>.</li>
  <li>The developer writes code and <strong>never commits</strong>.</li>
  <li>Code review and security review (OWASP framing) are <strong>gates, not opinions</strong>. A FAIL routes the change straight back to the developer with findings, and it goes round again.</li>
  <li>The tester is the <strong>only agent allowed to commit</strong>, and only once both gates pass and the suite is green.</li>
  <li>The documenter runs last, against what actually shipped.</li>
</ul>

<p>I work the change out with Claude in plan mode, approve it, and only then hand it to the project manager.</p>

<h3>Running alongside Matt Pocock&rsquo;s skills</h3>

<p><a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills">Matt Pocock&rsquo;s skills</a> sit underneath and supply the engineering habits: test-driven development (TDD), domain modelling and deep-module design.</p>

<p>I have come to especially appreciate the benefits of Matt&rsquo;s grillme skill. Grillme ensures that the LLM and I understand the task the same way before it starts coding.</p>

<hr>

<h2>Stage 02 &mdash; Delete what should never have existed</h2>

<p>Models are generous in their output. If you ask for a function, you probably will get an interface, a factory and three helpers that are never called twice. None of that is helpful.</p>

<p>Dietrich Gebert&rsquo;s <a href="https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail">ponytail</a> is the cure for this behaviour. Does a piece of code need to exist at all? Is it already in the codebase? Does the standard library do it? Is there a native platform feature? Can it be one line? New code is only allowed as a last resort.</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>/ponytail-review</code> on the diff, before human review, to catch what this change just added.</li>
  <li><code>/ponytail-audit</code> across the whole repo, periodically, for what has accumulated: dead code, duplicated blocks, and the one I care about most &mdash; <strong>near-twin abstractions</strong>, two utilities doing one job under different names, drifting slowly apart.</li>
  <li><code>/ponytail-debt</code> collects the shortcuts the pipeline took on purpose, so &ldquo;we&rsquo;ll do that later&rdquo; is a list rather than a feeling.</li>
</ul>

<p>It also runs as a live mode during implementation, which is a great deal cheaper than deleting fluff afterward.</p>

<hr>

<h2>Stage 03 &mdash; Measure it, then let a human read the top of the list</h2>

<p>My <a href="https://github.com/prototypo/codequalityexplorer">codequalityexplorer</a> ranks every finding worst-first into a CODE_QUALITY.md report, then fixes exactly one at a time. I cannot review everything the pipeline produced. I can review the worst things in it, and I know they are the worst because something measured them instead of sampling them.</p>

<p><code>/quality-report</code> measures the codebase using old-fashioned software engineering concepts including cyclomatic complexity, function length, nesting depth, argument counts, duplicated blocks, dead code, error-handling smells (bare <code>except</code>, Rust <code>unwrap</code>, swallowed catches), oversized files, documentation and reusability &mdash; and writes <code>CODE_QUALITY.md</code>: every finding, ranked worst-first. It never edits code.</p>

<p>Currently, Python, Rust, JavaScript and TypeScript are supported languages.</p>

<p><code>/quality-improve</code> takes exactly one finding, the top open one, fixes it, runs the tests, and stops. That constraint is deliberate. Nobody wants a tool that promises to fix everything but hands me back an unreviewable diff, which is the problem I started with.</p>

<p>The skill will ask you if you want to start a new branch for the improvements. Say yes.</p>

<p>You run this command again to tackle the next finding.</p>

<hr>

<h2>Clean up Claude&rsquo;s English</h2>

<p>A pipeline this size produces a lot of reports. Six agents, two gate verdicts, an over-engineering pass and a ranked quality file &mdash; all of it lands on me at the end of a long day. LLM prose makes that worse, especially with the newer Claude models.</p>

<p>The easy solution is to set an output style and hold every agent to it. Mine is <strong>ELI5</strong> crossed with <strong>ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English</strong> a limited vocabulary for technical reporting. It has a fixed approved vocabulary, one meaning per word, active voice, and one instruction or idea per sentence.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Plain words only.</strong> If a technical term is unavoidable, it gets explained straight after.</li>
  <li><strong>Active voice, short sentences, short paragraphs.</strong> No throat-clearing.</li>
  <li><strong>Report the outcome:</strong> what was done, whether it worked, what I do next.</li>
  <li><strong>Decisions come as two options, not five</strong> &mdash; with just enough context to choose, and a recommendation.</li>
  <li><strong>Paths and commands stay exact.</strong> Simplification applies to the prose, never to an identifier.</li>
</ul>

<p>These are not cosmetic preferences. Ambiguous review findings are how a real problem gets waved through, and the discipline that keeps a maintenance manual unambiguous does the same job for a code review. It also makes the gates faster to read, which means I actually read them.</p>

<hr>

<h2>Let Claude clean itself</h2>

<p>Every Claude upgrade changes what the model does well unaided, and scaffolding that helped last quarter can actively get in the way this quarter. You have probably seen Anthropic&rsquo;s guidance to delete your CLAUDE.md every quarter.</p>

<p>I run <code>/doctor</code> once per month. Anthropic has improved that tool immensely so it is worth doing. It will guide you through the process of complying with their best practices.</p>

